Greenfield Local Hub
Greenfield Local Hub is a web‑based marketplace that connects customers with local farmers and producers. The platform allows customers to browse products, place orders, and manage accounts, while producers can upload products, manage stock levels, and view incoming orders.
The system is built with a strong focus on security, data integrity, and role‑based access control, using Supabase Authentication and Row Level Security (RLS).

Key Features
Customer Features

Secure registration and login using Supabase Authentication
Browse available products uploaded by local producers
Add products to a shopping cart and manage quantities
Place orders and view full order history
Manage personal account details and loyalty points

Producer Features

Secure producer registration and login
Upload new products with images stored in Supabase Storage
Manage stock quantities through a producer dashboard
View incoming orders that include their products
Access limited customer information required for order fulfilment


Technologies Used

Frontend: HTML, CSS, JavaScript
Backend & Database: Supabase (PostgreSQL)
Authentication: Supabase Auth
Storage: Supabase Storage (public product images)
Security: Supabase Row Level Security (RLS)


Authentication Design
Supabase Authentication is used to manage all user registration and login functionality for both customers and producers.
Each authenticated user is assigned a unique identifier (auth_user_id) by Supabase. This identifier is used as a secure link between the authentication system and application‑specific profile tables.
Authentication responsibilities include:

Validating user credentials securely
Managing user sessions
Providing user identity via auth.uid()

Sensitive information such as passwords is never stored or handled directly by the application.

Row Level Security (RLS)
Row Level Security (RLS) is implemented across all application tables to ensure that users can only access data they are authorised to view or modify.
RLS policies are defined at the database level, meaning that even if client‑side checks were bypassed, the database itself would still prevent unauthorised access.
RLS Strategy
Each user‑specific table includes an auth_user_id column, which links records to the authenticated Supabase user.
Policies ensure that:

Customers can only view and update their own profile data
Producers can only manage products they own
Orders are only visible to the customer who placed them or the producer fulfilling them
Stock records can only be updated by the producer who owns the product

This approach enforces least‑privilege access, significantly improving system security.

Data Privacy & Protection
The system follows data minimisation principles.
Email addresses are managed exclusively by Supabase Authentication and are not duplicated across application tables unless explicitly required. This avoids unnecessary exposure of sensitive data.
Customer information visible to producers is intentionally limited to:

Customer reference ID
Optional first name
This provides sufficient information for order fulfilment without exposing private contact details.

Public producer information is stored separately from authentication data and is explicitly shared by the producer during registration.

Database Structure Overview

tbl_customer – Customer profile information
tbl_producer – Producer profile information
tbl_product – Product listings uploaded by producers
tbl_stock – Stock quantities for each product
tbl_cart – Active customer carts
tbl_cartitem – Products contained within carts
tbl_order – Placed orders
tbl_orderitem – Items associated with orders

All profile tables reference Supabase users via auth_user_id.

Product & Stock Management
Producers can upload products and images via the producer dashboard.

Product images are uploaded to Supabase Storage
Public URLs are generated and stored in the database
Stock levels are tracked separately
Validation ensures stock cannot fall below zero

This ensures accurate inventory management and prevents customers from ordering unavailable items.

Order Processing
Orders are processed in multiple secure steps:

Customer places an order
Order record is created
Associated order items are recorded
Stock levels are updated
Customer cart is cleared

Customers can view their full order history, while producers can view only orders containing their products.

Error Handling & User Feedback
The system includes defensive programming techniques to improve reliability:

Client‑side input validation
User‑friendly error and success messages
Graceful handling of empty or missing data
Fallback messages when content is unavailable

This improves the user experience and prevents invalid operations.

How to Run the Project

Clone or download the project files
Configure Supabase credentials in supabaseClient.js
Ensure Supabase tables and RLS policies are set up correctly
Serve the project using a local web server
Open index.html to begin


Future Improvements

